  family: { fn: renderFamilyTab, fallback: "家庭系统加载中..." },
  personal_growth: { fn: renderPersonalGrowthTab, fallback: "个人成长系统加载中..." },

  // Cross-file (look up dynamically):
  investment: { fnName: "renderInvestmentTab", fallback: "投资系统加载中..." },
  startup: { fnName: "renderStartupTab", fallback: "创业系统加载中..." },
  wiki: { fnName: "renderWikiTab", fallback: "📖 百科系统加载中..." },
};
```

And update the call site:
```javascript
const renderer = TAB_RENDERERS[currentTab];
if (typeof renderer === "function") {
  renderer(state, area);
} else if (renderer) {
  var actualFn = renderer.fn;
  if (typeof actualFn !== "function" && renderer.fnName) {
    actualFn = window[renderer.fnName];
  }
  if (typeof actualFn === "function") {
    actualFn(state, area);
  } else {
    area.innerHTML += '<p style="...">' + (renderer.fallback || "开发中...") + "</p>";
  }
} else {
  area.innerHTML += '<p style="...">📌 开发中...</p>';
}
```

Actually, this is getting overly complex. The real fix is much simpler.

The REAL issue is: when `TAB_RENDERERS` is created, some function references point to `undefined` because those functions are defined in other JS files that haven't loaded yet.

The CLEANEST fix: I could also define the registry AFTER all scripts have loaded, but that's hard in a non-module system.

Alternatively: just move the `{ fn: fnName }` pattern to look up at call time. But the simplest fix that works with the current code is:

Instead of storing the function reference when the registry is created (when cross-file functions don't exist yet), store a zero-argument function that returns the current value:

```javascript
const TAB_RENDERERS = {
  // Cross-file functions - use getter to ensure we use the latest value
  investment: { getFn: function() { return typeof renderInvestmentTab === 'function' ? renderInvestmentTab : null; }, fallback: "..." },
};
```

But that's ugly too.

OK, let me just use the cleanest approach: **store function names for cross-file references and look them up via `window` at call time.**

This is what I'll do. Let me make the edits.

